# The Discrete Extension Principle

> **READ FIRST — what this principle is and is not.** A first-class
> framework principle (peer to the Basepoint Principle
> (`basepoint_principle.md`) and the `substrate_determinism.md`
> inviolables), elevating the multiply-verified pattern in which an
> apparatus gap closes via a *substrate-aligned discrete primitive*
> built from existing inviolables rather than continuous-parameter
> Hilbert-space machinery.
>
> **Its heart is a discriminator, not a licence.** It is **NOT**
> "every apparatus gap admits a substrate-aligned closure" — only
> those for which a discrete primitive can be *exhibited*. Absent
> a substrate-aligned primitive after careful search, the gap is
> structural (the substrate's reach really does stop there).
> Misreading this as a blanket expectation that combinatorial
> closures should always be findable is the failure mode this
> principle exists to prevent.

## Statement

**When the substrate's bare apparatus underdetermines a target —
whether a Class-2 empirical leaf, a narrowing finding, or a
substitution-scope caveat — the framework should search for a
substrate-aligned discrete primitive that closes the gap from
substrate combinatorics built on existing inviolables. Such a
primitive *closes* the gap iff it is (a) discrete-or-combinatorial
(not continuous-parameter), (b) derived from existing substrate
primitives (not postulated), and (c) substrate-native (not borrowed
from QM with relabelling). If found, the closure is *structural*
(promotes the leaf one Class level). If despite careful search no
such primitive can be exhibited, the gap is structurally genuine
— the substrate's reach really does stop at that boundary, and
that boundary is itself a structural fact.**

The principle composes with the Basepoint Principle: both have the
same discriminator shape — *feature* or *open* decided by whether
the relevant obstruction (Basepoint: no equivariant selector;
Discrete Extension: no substrate-aligned discrete primitive) can
be *exhibited*. Neither is a blanket excuse to stop deriving.

## The discriminator (the operative core — never omit it)

| Absence of a substrate closure is… | …then the gap is |
|---|---|
| **structurally bridgeable** — a substrate-aligned discrete primitive can be exhibited that closes the gap from existing inviolables via combinatorial reasoning | a **derivation route** (close it; promote Class level) |
| **structurally non-bridgeable** — careful search establishes that no substrate-aligned discrete primitive exists (the gap requires continuous-parameter or QM-substitution machinery) | a **structural boundary** (declare it; document the boundary; report what would extend) |
| **operational** — no exhaustive search performed; gap may admit closure not yet found | **genuinely open** (keep searching; no closure-by-default) |

The principle is invoked **only** in the first two rows; the third
row keeps the framework honest. A "structural boundary" finding
requires the boundary to be *demonstrated*, not merely asserted —
typically by exhibiting candidate primitives and ruling each out,
or by showing a continuous-parameter dependence that no discrete
primitive can absorb.

