## One-line summary

The **Discrete Extension Principle** elevates the multiply-verified
pattern (K(t) cadence closure #179, T1a Dicke extension #187, D3
Bell bounds substitution-scope tightening #188) into a first-class
framework discriminator-clause principle, peer to the Basepoint
Principle: when the substrate's bare apparatus underdetermines a
target (Class-2 empirical leaf, narrowing finding, substitution-scope
caveat), search for a substrate-aligned *discrete combinatorial
primitive* built from existing inviolables that closes the gap; if
found and audit-disciplined (separating substrate-derived
components from foundational-layer load), the gap is structurally
bridgeable and the leaf promotes Class level; if despite careful
search no such primitive can be exhibited and the gap is shown to
require continuous-parameter or QM-substitution machinery, the
gap is a *structural boundary* (e.g., the SNMC boundary at
non-stabilizer non-Dicke states verified in #187 batch-2 tests);
candidate open framework items where the principle may deploy
include the Salpeter slope (currently Class-2 dual-gate) and the
Cl(3, 1) anticommutation derivation; the principle is *not* a
licence to expect closure-by-default and *not* a prescription
forbidding continuous-parameter substrate primitives — it is a
discipline for closure judgments, with the structural verdict
requiring the obstruction or closure to be *exhibited* rather
than asserted; the principle composes with the Basepoint Principle
(declined-basepoint discrimination) and with the
`substrate_determinism.md` inviolables to form a binary
discriminator system covering most framework gaps; ready for
deployment on remaining Class-2/Class-3 leaves and as the
methodological spine for **B1 (programmatic QM-reframing doc)**.
